BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"North Bay Bohemian": "https://bohemian.com/events-calendar/#/details/racial-wealth-gap-learning-simulation/18292791/2026-03-21T14"}
X-SOURCE-ID:bohemian
SUMMARY:Racial Wealth Gap Learning Simulation
DTSTART;TZID=PDT;VALUE=DATE-TIME:20260321T140000
DTEND;TZID=PDT;VALUE=DATE-TIME:20260321T160000
UID:260309LSW_oLuYj0SR5QJSEiQyAw
DESCRIPTION:This free event is hosted by United in Grace Lutheran Church o
 f Vallejo and the NAACP Vallejo Branch No. 1081\n\nThe Racial Wealth Gap L
 earning Simulation\, developed by Bread for the World and NETWORK\, is an 
 interactive exercise that explores how 13 historical federal policies in t
 he United States created structural inequalities that persist today.\n\nHo
 w the Simulation Works\n\nThe simulation typically lasts about one hour an
 d is designed for diverse groups\, including faith organizations\, schools
 \, and nonprofits. \n\nRole Play: Participants are randomly assigned a ra
 cial identity \\(often different from their own\\) to break down personal 
 biases and focus on structural impacts.\n\nMechanics: Players move through
  a timeline of 13 federal policies. Each policy card dictates actions—ga
 ining or losing "money\," "land\," or "lost opportunity" cards—based on 
 the participant’s assigned race.\n\nOutcome: By the end\, the physical d
 istribution of cards mirrors the actual racial wealth divide in the U.S. t
 oday\, where white households have significantly higher median net worth t
 han Black households.\n\nDiscussion: The session concludes with a facilita
 ted group discussion to process emotions and identify ways to apply a "rac
 ial equity lens" to advocacy and community work.\n\nKey Educational Focus\
 n\nThe simulation highlights how policy \\(not just individual choice\\) d
 rives hunger and poverty. It covers influential areas such as: \n\nProper
 ty Ownership: Policies that historically barred or limited land and home o
 wnership for communities of color.\n\nEducation: Systemic barriers to educ
 ational access and funding.\n\nSocial Safety Nets: How various federal pro
 grams were historically structured to exclude or under-serve certain racia
 l groups.
LOCATION:Vallejo Naval and Historical Museum
URL:https://bohemian.com/events-calendar/#/details/racial-wealth-gap-learn
 ing-simulation/18292791/2026-03-21T14
X-SOURCE:North Bay Bohemian
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
